WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.080
OK.

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:02.550
Let's get right into audience questions.

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:05.540
The first one is about Mike Pence.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:09.670
David Wood replacing Mike Pence help Donald
Trump get reelected.

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:11.549
Would it help him in the polls?

00:00:11.549 --> 00:00:17.130
So it's extremely unlikely that replacing
Pence would help Trump.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.710
And it has the serious potential to hurt Donald
Trump.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:25.640
And there's a few things you have to understand
about V.P. picks, V.P. picks, the situation.

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:30.920
The risk reward with the vice presidential
pick is asymmetrical.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:37.540
And what I mean by that is a bad V.P. pick
can hurt you much more than a good V.P. pick

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:43.850
can help you because a good V.P. pick essentially
causes no drama, causes no scandals, stays

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:50.980
out of the way, is a reasonably OK debater
so that in the not particularly highly viewed

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:55.260
vice presidential debate, they hold their
own and they essentially just they know the

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:56.260
talking points.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:57.570
They don't get hugely embarrassed.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:03.940
You know, Sarah Palin in her V.P. debate really
didn't do well, but it still didn't really

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:05.320
hurt McCain Palin that much.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:08.900
I mean, it already wasn't going particularly
well for them.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:15.500
But on the other hand, if you have a great
V.P., they don't do that much for you.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:23.160
And so that's why the ace asymmetrical nature
to me would suggest that unless pense is a

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:27.120
problem for Trump, it's risky to replace him.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:33.880
And you also have to remember replacing your
V.P. is a sign that something is wrong, that

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:39.920
the very news of replacing the V.P. if Trump
were to do it, even if he takes Pence and

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.950
replaces him with someone like equally.

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:45.780
Okay, that could be a wash.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:51.479
But the news that there is chaos and disorganization
with, you know, were days before the convention

00:01:51.479 --> 00:01:57.060
at this point, the Republican National Convention
that could actually hurt Donald Trump.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:04.670
And there if we look at Pence specifically,
you could also make the case that he's complimentary

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:06.030
to Trump in some way.

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:11.920
I know that Trump now pretends to be religious
and he says when he was in his late 60s, he

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:16.380
stopped being pro-choice and now he's against
abortion and all of this stuff.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:17.630
You know, he reads the Bible.

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:18.680
I don't believe any of it.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.760
But to the extent that anybody does or doesn't
believe it.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.730
Mike Pence is evangelical critic.

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:32.430
Credentials probably help Trump in some key
states with the evangelical base, which, again,

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:33.430
white evangelicals.

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:35.120
We'll talk about this later, actually.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:37.720
White evangelicals tend to vote Republican.

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.870
Would they be turned off from Trump if Pence
wasn't there?

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:41.870
I don't know.

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:49.959
So to me, a bad V.P can really be a problem,
whereas a good V.P. just kind of keeps chugging

00:02:49.959 --> 00:02:50.959
along.

00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:57.420
Pence, in terms of demographics, could actually
be good for Trump and a replacement itself

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.450
could signal trouble and could backfire.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:06.120
Now, the one thing I am interested in that's
that's maybe a little bit different this time

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:17.360
around is we are going to have a Democratic
female V.P. candidate debating a white evangelical

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:20.940
older male Republican in the V.P. debate.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:32.700
Now, if you think back to Palin versus Biden
back in 2008, Sarah Palin is just not as quick

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:39.530
or or well versed in policy or skilled at
debating as is Kamala Harris.

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:48.910
But the dynamics were where you had the Democratic
white male debating against the younger Republican

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:49.910
woman.

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:56.410
And that is a different dynamic, particularly
in these times where xenophobia is now welcomed

00:03:56.410 --> 00:03:59.860
